 filed history. CEO compensation is 5\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags moderate compensation governance risk (5/10).","financial_summary":["Babcock & Wilcox Enterprises, I is a small-cap industrials business with a $663M market cap on $588M of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at -13% over the past three years and -10% annually across the full decade, and a shrinking top line is the hardest headwind any business can overcome.","Returns on capital run at -10% against a 11% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests, and -11% gross margins are typical of commodity economics where competitors can replicate the product cheaply.","The company is burning through $86M of cash, and without positive owner earnings any valuation rests on hope rather than evidence.","On the balance sheet, the balance sheet carries net cash, which is the gold standard: the business never has to make desperate decisions in a bad year, and thin 0.6\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Net dilution runs at 19.6% annually, transferring value from owners to stock-based comp, and insiders hold 3.4% of the shares, and profit capture is only 0.3\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Piotroski score 1/9 \u2014 the fundamentals are deteriorating on multiple fronts","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (-1.9) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"ROIC sits in the bottom decile among Industrials peers \u2014 no moat advantage to speak of","sev":"red","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Revenue growth in the bottom decile of Industrials peers \u2014 losing share inside the profit pool","sev":"red","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev"